---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Story from '../../components/Story.astro';
import Grid from '../../components/Grid.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import roof from '../../assets/photos/roof-inspection-drone.jpg';
import briefing from '../../assets/photos/storm-briefing.jpg';
import tornadoes from '../../assets/photos/milton-tornado-tracks.jpg';
import Video from '../../components/Video.astro';
import { VIDEOS } from '../../data/videos';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Hurricane Milton, 2024"
  description="How the Florida Lantern Project responded to Hurricane Milton — 20+ people placed in emergency lodging, free eSIM phone plans, and free roof inspections across Tampa Bay."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="October 2024"
    title="Hurricane Milton"
    lede="Nine days after Helene. Some families were hit twice before anyone had assessed the first round of damage."
  />

  <Section title="Before landfall: somewhere to go">
    <p class="intro">
      On 6 October, three days before landfall, we secured discounted rooms at a
      Hilton in Orlando — $68 a night, breakfast and parking included — and
      offered them publicly.
    </p>
    <p>
      That post was shared 700 times. It was the most-shared thing this
      organization has ever published, and it wasn't an appeal. It was an offer.
    </p>
    <p>
      We placed <strong>more than 20 people</strong> in emergency lodging in the
      days before the storm. Pets included. That detail is why several of them
      called us at all.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" title="The people">
    <Grid min="320px">
      <Story name="Peter" context="Evacuated a dorm · one cat">
        <p>
          A college student ordered out of their dorm, with no family nearby and a
          cat no shelter would accept. They told us they were planning to ride out
          the storm in their car.
        </p>
        <p>We placed them both in a hotel the same day.</p>
        <blockquote>
          My cat and I have only been able to safely make it through Hurricane
          Milton because of the help given to us by this organization.
        </blockquote>
      </Story>
      <Story name="Amy" context="Hit by Helene, then Milton · six of them">
        <p>
          A single mother with two kids, two cats and a dog. Helene had already
          flooded her house and totalled her car. Milton arrived before an adjuster
          could even visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          No car, no backup plan, nowhere to go. We arranged transport and lodging
          for the entire household — all six of them — more than a hundred miles
          inland, hours before landfall.
        </p>
        <p>No forms. No waitlist. No delay.</p>
      </Story>
      <Story name="Maria" context="First-time homeowner · roof damage">
        <p>
          Maria had owned her first home for less than a year when Milton took
          most of her roof.
        </p>
        <p>
          We inspected it, wrote the report, and told her about SBA disaster loans
          — which she hadn't known were open to homeowners. The loan she secured
          covered the damage and funded mitigation work, at under 4% with no
          payments for a year.
        </p>
      </Story>
    </Grid>
    <Video video={VIDEOS.roadToSafety} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="During: staying reachable">
    <p>
      On 8 October we offered free one-month unlimited talk and text plans for
      anyone with an eSIM-compatible phone. Cell service was expected to fail, and
      a family separated across an evacuation route with a dead phone is a
      different kind of emergency.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also ran a non-emergency storm hotline: radar, forecasts, shelter status,
      and the specific questions official lines can't take.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={briefing}
      alt="A storm briefing graphic over a surge forecast map of Florida, explaining that the storm is perpendicular to the coast and that the area just south of the eye will see catastrophic surge."
      caption="One of the plain-language briefings we published as Milton approached."
      ratio="3 / 4"
    />
    <ul class="quotes">
      <li>“Can I still leave if I'm in Zone B?”</li>
      <li>“My window is cracked. What do I do?”</li>
      <li>“My daughter's on a ventilator, and I'm staying. How can I keep the power on?”</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Milton's other hazard">
    <p class="intro">
      Milton is remembered for the surge, but it spun up an extraordinary
      tornado outbreak across the peninsula on the way in — including well east
      of the track, in places that expected wind and rain and got something else.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={tornadoes}
      alt="A map of Florida overlaid with dozens of red tornado warning polygons stretching from the Gulf coast across to the Atlantic."
      caption="Tornado warnings during Milton. Every red box is a separate warning."
      ratio="1 / 1"
    />
    <p>
      It is the reason our storm updates covered more than the cone. A family
      three counties inland, told they were outside the surge zone, still needed
      to know what was overhead.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" title="After: documentation">
    <p class="intro">
      On 11 October we began offering free roof and home inspections across the
      Tampa Bay area. We completed <strong>dozens</strong>.
    </p>
    <Figure
      src={roof}
      alt="Overhead drone photograph of a Spanish tile roof with displaced and broken tiles."
      caption="One of dozens of properties surveyed across Tampa Bay after Milton."
    />
    <p>
      An inspection is a drone flight, flown under FAA Part 107, and a written
      report by a Florida licensed home inspector. What it's actually for is the
      moment an insurer asks a family to prove what the damage replaced —
      and for pointing people at aid programs nobody told them about. That second
      part turned out to matter as much as the first.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The totals">
    <ul class="totals">
      <li><strong>20+</strong><span>placed in emergency lodging before landfall</span></li>
      <li><strong>Dozens</strong><span>of free roof and property inspections</span></li>
      <li><strong>50+</strong><span>individuals and families assisted across both storms</span></li>
      <li><strong>$0</strong><span>operating budget</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Delivered through donated hotel capacity, volunteer labour, and partners who
      answered the phone. That worked once. It is not a plan.
      <a href="/give/lantern-keeper">This is why we ask for monthly support.</a>
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .intro { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .quotes { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--space-md) 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-sm); }
  .quotes li { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--step-1); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding-left: var(--space-sm); }
  .totals { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--space-md) 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-md); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(180px, 100%), 1fr)); }
  .totals li { border-top: 3px solid var(--color-accent); padding-top: var(--space-sm); }
  .totals strong { display: block; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1; }
  .totals span { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: var(--step--1); }
  .note { max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
